    "created_at": "2016-04-11T13:46:17Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-04-11T13:46:17Z",
    "body": "In Gecko, the \"oscpu\" value is what gets sent in the UA string.  The \"platform\" value is also something that gets sent in the UA string (and is NOT the same generally speaking as oscpu), except on Mac and Windows where it's something harcoded.\n\nSo on Mac for me, for example, \"platform\" is \"MacIntel\", \"oscpu\" is \"Intel Mac OS X 10.10\" and \"userAgent\" is \"Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10.10; rv:48.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/48.0\".\n\nFor comparison, in Safari \"platform\" is \"MacIntel\" and \"userAgent\" is \"Mozilla/5.0 (Macintosh; Intel Mac OS X 10_10_5) AppleWebKit/601.5.17 (KHTML, like Gecko) Version/9.1 Safari/601.5.17\".\n\nAnd in Chrome it's basically the same thing, but with the \"Chrome/\" bit added and a different \"AppleWebKit/\" version and the \"Version/9.1\" removed.\n\nSo in other words, \"oscpu\" looks nothing like \"platform\" there.  But the relevant value is already available to some extent, in that it's being put in the UA string anyway...\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
